  "output": "The incident analysis reveals a classic case of cascading failure driven by an external upstream constraint interacting poorly with local client-side retry logic. The timeline begins with a stable state at 09:00 (E01), where the system maintained a healthy queue depth of 120 with 8 workers. The critical inflection point occurred between 09:10 and 09:15. During this window, the queue depth exploded by over 1400% to 1,850 tasks (E02), coinciding with a nearly 800% increase in median task latency (from 1.8s to 14.2s) (E03).\n\nCrucially, host-level resource monitoring at 09:14 showed CPU utilization at only 43% and memory at 51% (E04). This data point is vital for diagnosis; it definitively rules out local resource exhaustion (CPU-bound or memory-pressure Garbage Collection) as the bottleneck. The system was not struggling to compute, but rather waiting. The \"wait\" was identified at 09:15 when Dependency B began returning HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests) errors for 31% of outbound calls (E05). This indicates that the upstream provider throttled our traffic.\n\nHowever, the throttle alone does not explain the magnitude of the queue growth. The primary amplifier was the client-side retry strategy. At 09:16, retry volume surged by 420% (E06). With an exponential backoff policy capped at three retries, every single request that hit the 429 limit generated up to three additional requests, all of which were also likely to be throttled. This created a \"thundering herd\" or \"retry storm,\" where the system consumed its own capacity attempting to recover from failures it could not immediately fix.\n\nThe ineffectiveness of horizontal scaling further supports this diagnosis. When workers were doubled from 8 to 16 at 09:20, the queue depth decreased by a negligible 6% over ten minutes (E07). If the issue were computational, doubling compute should have halved the lag. Since it did not, the constraint remained external—the additional workers simply generated more concurrent requests to the already-blocked Dependency B, potentially exacerbating the rate limiting.\n\nDefinitive proof of the retry storm's impact was provided at 09:26 when retries were disabled for Dependency B. The queue depth plummeted by 38% in just six minutes (E08). This rapid clearance occurred because the system stopped multiplying the workload and began processing new tasks or failing fast, allowing the existing backlog to drain. Finally, once the root cause was addressed—upstream rate limits were raised at 09:35—the error rate dropped below 1%, and the queue normalized within eleven minutes (E09). Throughout this period, no internal infrastructure changes were detected (E10), isolating the fault domain entirely to the interaction between the service and Dependency B.\n\nIn conclusion, the primary cause was upstream rate limiting (Dependency B), but the severe impact duration was caused by aggressive retry policies that acted as a positive feedback loop. Remediation must focus on breaking this loop via circuit breakers and adaptive concurrency control rather than relying solely on autoscaling.",
